    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="aquarm-sdk" tabindex="-1">AQUARM SDK <a class="header-anchor" href="#aquarm-sdk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AQUARM SDK&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The <a href="https://github.com/kalman-kr/aquarm_sdk" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">AQUARM SDK</a> provides C++ and Python interfaces for robot kinematics, trajectory generation, and KCAN communication.</p><h2 id="components" tabindex="-1">Components <a class="header-anchor" href="#components" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Components&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Component</th><th style="text-align:left;">Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Robot model</td><td style="text-align:left;">Load a root-to-tip chain from a URDF with <code>revolute</code>, <code>continuous</code>, and <code>fixed</code> joints</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Kinematics</td><td style="text-align:left;">Compute forward kinematics, Jacobians, and damped least-squares inverse kinematics</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Trajectory</td><td style="text-align:left;">Generate quintic joint trajectories between start and goal configurations</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">KCAN</td><td style="text-align:left;">Connect to the controller, read joint feedback, and send joint commands</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">MuJoCo</td><td style="text-align:left;">Simulate the arm and gripper using the bundled MJCF model</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The C++ API is exposed through <code>kalman_ik.hpp</code> and <code>kcan_sdk_user.h</code>. Python examples use the bundled <code>kalman_ik_py</code> and <code>kcan_sdk_user_py</code> modules.</p><h2 id="command-flow" tabindex="-1">Command Flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#command-flow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Command Flow&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The supplied IK examples control one arm through a direct KCAN connection:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Target pose + URDF model</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Current joint feedback → IK solution</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Quintic joint trajectory</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>KCAN SDK → Controller</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The examples connect to <code>192.168.1.9</code> using ports <code>20000</code> and <code>20001</code>, request position mode, and send joint targets approximately every 5 ms. Adjust the connection settings in the example source to match your controller.</p><p>Positions are expressed in meters and joint angles in radians. The twist/Jacobian component order is <code>[linear; angular]</code>.</p><h2 id="robot-descriptions" tabindex="-1">Robot Descriptions <a class="header-anchor" href="#robot-descriptions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Robot Descriptions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="https://github.com/kalman-kr/aquarm_description" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">AQUARM Description repository</a> contains URDF/Xacro models, meshes, RViz configuration, and ROS 2 viewers. The SDK examples load their bundled model from <code>resources/urdf/aquarm_origin_with_gripper.urdf</code>.</p><h2 id="getting-started" tabindex="-1">Getting Started <a class="header-anchor" href="#getting-started" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Getting Started&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./../start_guide/sdk_guide">SDK Guide</a>: requirements, build commands, and example code.</li><li><a href="./ros">ROS</a>: description package installation and RViz visualization.</li><li><a href="./../resources/download">Download</a>: links to both repositories.</li></ul>', 14)
